<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indo-Greek Kingdom</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Indo-Greek</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indo-Greek Kingdom was a group of Hellenistic states in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, ruled by Greek kings from the second century BCE. It developed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom</a>. Its fusion of Greek and Indian culture, seen in Gandharan art, is part of the legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom.jpg" alt="Indo-Greek Kingdom" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Hippostratos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kujula then crossed the Hindu Kush, took the Kabul region and Gandhara from the Indo-Parthian successors of Gondophares and absorbed the last <b>Indo-Greek</b> and Saka principalities; some of his early coins imitate those of the Indo-Greek king Hermaeus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Bactria the Greek settler population gave rise to the Greco-Bactrian and later <b>Indo-Greek</b> kingdoms, which outlived Seleucid rule in the east and contributed to the cultural synthesis later visible in Gandharan Buddhist art, where the conventions of Greek sculpture were adapted to represent the Buddha and bodhisattvas,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdoms his generals carved out of his conquests, the Ptolemies in Egypt, the Seleucids in Syria, Mesopotamia, and Iran, and later the Greek kings of Bactria and of <b>north-western India</b>, ruled for generations as Greek-speaking dynasties over peoples who were not Greek.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Demetrius I the kings crossed the Hindu Kush into India, founding the <b>Indo-Greek</b> kingdoms, while Eucratides I ruled a vast territory in the 2nd century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Indo-Parthian kings continued <b>Indo-Greek</b> and Saka traditions of bilingual coinage with Greek and Kharoshthi legends and ruled through governors and allied local dynasties such as the Apracharajas.</blockquote><a href="/state/indo-parthian-kingdom">Indo-Parthian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Indo-Scythian kings called themselves king of kings and copied <b>Indo-Greek</b> coinage, with Greek and Kharoshthi legends.</blockquote><a href="/state/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a></li></ul></section>
